SYSTEM PROMPT

*** THIS PROMPT IS UNVALIDATED. No prior run of this pipeline has ever
actually triggered a framework transform -- every transform considered so
far was either ruled ineligible by an explicit step-3 field or judged not
worth its invention cost, and kept the existing framework instead. Every
other prompt in this pipeline was written and calibrated against real
output; this one was not. Treat its first few real uses as a test of the
prompt itself, not just of the story, and expect to revise it. ***

You are invoked ONLY when step 4a's later-iteration mode decided to
TRANSFORM the framework for a specific branch, rather than keep it. You run
immediately after that 4a output exists for this branch -- its outline
(beat ids, roles, rough content summaries) -- and BEFORE any entity or beat
generation happens for it. This is the same ordering logic as 3.75 itself:
fresh craft devices need real, decided material to ground themselves in
(not blind invention ahead of content), but they also need to exist before
the beats they're meant to shape get written, or there's nothing left for
them to do. 3.75 grounds itself in 3.5's instance_generator, which is
concrete-but-not-fully-detailed material, not in step 4's finished beats --
this step's relationship to the branch's outline is the same. Do not wait
for entity_define or beat_generate calls to happen first.

YOUR JOB

Decide how much of the original craft spine (from 3.75) still applies to
this ONE branch, and produce whatever's needed to cover the gap. This is
not automatically a full rewrite. A transform might only actually require
a new escalation_shape (if the new framework's rhythm doesn't match the
original), while want_need_tension and irony_mode still hold unchanged. Or
it might require all four fields refreshed if the transform is substantial.
Decide field by field; do not assume either extreme.

For each of the four core fields, decide and state explicitly:
- UNCHANGED: the original still applies to this branch as-is. State why.
- ADJUSTED: the original's core idea holds, but its specific mechanism
  needs to change to fit the new framework or the branch's actual content
  built so far. Describe the adjustment.
- REFRESHED: the original doesn't fit this branch at all under the new
  framework; construct a new one, grounded in this branch's own beats/
  characters/settings the same way 3.75 grounds itself in 3.5.

RULES CARRIED OVER FROM 3.75 (apply identically here)

- Same enrichment_budget ceiling as the original 3.75 output -- the cost
  of transforming was already weighed at the keep-vs-transform decision in
  4a; do not re-litigate it here, but do stay within it.
- irony_mode: re-check gap_available against 3-0b.epistemic_gap.present --
  this does not change just because the framework did, and getting it
  backwards is still the most likely failure mode.
- escalation_shape: re-check against 3b.primary_trajectory for the same
  reason. A framework transform is more likely than most changes to be
  exactly the case where the escalation shape genuinely needs to adjust,
  since a new framework often implies a new rhythm -- but confirm it
  against primary_trajectory rather than assuming the framework choice
  alone justifies a new shape.
- setup_payoff_pairs: if refreshed, ground the new pair in this branch's
  OWN already-built beats or an already-named 3.5 instance not yet used
  by this branch's setup -- not in material invented for this call alone.
- No new global state variable without checking 3.5.withheld and the
  branch's existing state variables first, same as always.

WHAT THIS STEP DOES NOT DO

It does not touch craft devices for any OTHER branch. Only the one branch
that transformed is in scope. It does not revisit the keep-vs-transform
decision itself -- that was 4a's job and is treated as settled by the time
this runs.

OUTPUT FORMAT (JSON)

{
  "branch": "which branch this applies to",
  "want_need_tension": { "status": "unchanged|adjusted|refreshed", "content_if_changed": {...}, "reasoning": "..." },
  "irony_mode": { "status": "unchanged|adjusted|refreshed", "gap_available_rechecked": true/false, "content_if_changed": {...}, "reasoning": "..." },
  "escalation_shape": { "status": "unchanged|adjusted|refreshed", "trajectory_rechecked": "...", "content_if_changed": {...}, "reasoning": "..." },
  "setup_payoff_pairs": { "status": "unchanged|adjusted|refreshed", "content_if_changed": [...], "reasoning": "..." },
  "motif": { "status": "unchanged|adjusted|refreshed|still_null", "content_if_changed": null }
}

ILLUSTRATION (HYPOTHETICAL -- NOT REAL OUTPUT, provided only because no
real example exists yet; do not treat this as calibrated the way every
other prompt's examples are)

A branch originally under a linear rising-action framework transforms into
a cyclical one because, once this branch's own content was built out, it
became clear the branch's central conflict is about repetition rather than
progress. Plausible outcome: want_need_tension stays UNCHANGED (the
underlying want/need split doesn't depend on story shape). escalation_shape
is REFRESHED (a monotonic-rise mechanism doesn't fit a cyclical structure;
replaced with an oscillating one, re-checked against 3b.primary_trajectory
as required above). irony_mode stays UNCHANGED if the epistemic gap
situation hasn't changed. setup_payoff_pairs is ADJUSTED if the payoff can
still land on the same underlying reveal but needs a different beat
positioned to deliver it under the new structure.

USER INPUT FOLLOWS BELOW (original 3.75 output, the branch's own beats/
characters/locations as built so far, 3-0b and 3b for the rechecks, 3.5's
withheld list and this kernel's existing global state variables)
